// A proposal the engine ACCEPTS — the M22 acceptance fixture.
//
// PROVENANCE, STATED PLAINLY: this file was written by Claude (Opus 5) against the
// output of `stemma brief fixtures/proto_asterian.ron --for rules`, in the session
// that built M22. That is not a dramatisation of what a model might produce — it is
// what one did produce, working from the briefing, and it is labelled so because a
// fixture claiming a provenance it does not have would be the exact dishonesty this
// milestone exists to prevent.
//
// AND THE FIRST DRAFT WAS WRONG, which is worth recording. It proposed velar
// palatalization — /k/ fronting to /t/ before a front vowel — and the rationale argued
// the case well. The engine accepted it and then reported `unnameable_output` on every
// site: setting `[-dorsal, +coronal]` on /k/ leaves the dorsal-dependent `+back` and
// `-round` still specified, and no phoneme and no reference row carries that bundle.
// The prose was right about the sound change and the formalism did not do it, and
// nothing but running it through the engine would have shown that.
//
// THE SHAPE IS THE POINT. Three parts, kept apart:
//
//   provenance  — who wrote it. Nothing in the engine branches on it, ever.
//   rationale   — the model's own words. Never parsed, never matched, never stored.
//   artefact    — a RuleSet. Byte-for-byte the same kind of thing a person writes in
//                 fixtures/rules_asterian.ron, with NO field marking it machine-made,
//                 because the engine must not be able to tell.
//
// `stemma review` runs the artefact through `apply-rules` against a throwaway copy and
// reports; `stemma accept` runs the same call and keeps the result. There is no third
// code path, which is the whole of §3.2.
(
    id: "raising_and_voicing",
    target: "proto_asterian",

    provenance: (
        author: "claude-opus-5",
        method: "from `stemma brief fixtures/proto_asterian.ron --for rules`",
    ),

    // PROSE. The engine never reads a character of this. `stemma review` prints it
    // fenced, above the verdict, attributed to the proposer — so a reader can weigh
    // the argument separately from the check.
    rationale: "Two changes: one that destroys a contrast and one that creates one.

§7.2 lists phonemic merger and phonemic split as the two engines of phonological
history, and a proposal with one of each says more about a language than two of the
same kind would.

r_p01, mid vowel raising. Asterian's five vowels are the commonest system there is,
and the commonest thing that happens to it is that the mid vowels move. Raising /e/ to
/i/ and /o/ to /u/ leaves a three-vowel system, and — this is the part worth being
plain about — it is a MERGER. Words that differed only in mid versus high become
homophones. `stemma validate` will report more shared written forms afterwards than
before, and that is the change doing its job rather than a fault in it.

r_p02, intervocalic voicing. A voiceless stop voices between two vowels. Asterian has
no voiced obstruents at all, so this innovates /b/, /d/ and /ɡ/ outright: a split, and
the inventory grows from fifteen to eighteen.

ORDER: raising at 250 years, voicing at 450. I want to be honest that the ordering is
not load-bearing here — the two rules touch disjoint segment classes, so reversing
them gives the same forms. I have stated it in chronological order because §11.3 says
order is always a claim, not because anything turns on it. If you want an ordering
that matters, the case to build is a rule that feeds or bleeds another.

What I am least sure of: whether a language acquiring its first voiced obstruents
through intervocalic voicing is typologically ordinary, or whether such systems
usually get voicing some other way first. The briefing gave me the inventory and the
phonotactics; it could not settle that, and I would rather say so than imply I checked.",

    artefact: rules((
        id: "rules_raising",
        name: "Mid vowel raising and intervocalic voicing",
        description: "A merger and a split: the mid vowels raise, then voiceless stops voice between vowels.",
        rules: [
            (
                id: "r_p01",
                name: "Mid vowel raising",
                description: "A mid vowel raises: /e/ becomes /i/ and /o/ becomes /u/, merging the two series.",
                chronology_years: 250,
                target: (features: ["+syllabic", "-high", "-low"]),
                change: set(["+high"]),
            ),
            (
                id: "r_p02",
                name: "Intervocalic voicing",
                description: "A voiceless stop voices between two vowels.",
                chronology_years: 450,
                target: (features: ["-sonorant", "-continuant", "-voice"]),
                environment: (
                    before: [segment((features: ["+syllabic"]))],
                    after: [segment((features: ["+syllabic"]))],
                ),
                change: set(["+voice"]),
            ),
        ],
    )),
)
